---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import LifeCycleSim from '../../simulators/lifecycle/LifeCycleSim.astro';
import { lifeCycleQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/lifecycle';

const toc = [
  { id: 'why', label: 'Why reproduction matters' },
  { id: 'sexual-asexual', label: 'Sexual and asexual reproduction' },
  { id: 'fertilisation', label: 'Fertilisation and early development' },
  { id: 'metamorphosis', label: 'Metamorphosis in insects' },
  { id: 'frog', label: 'The frog’s double life' },
  { id: 'chicken', label: 'Birds and the shelled egg' },
  { id: 'birth', label: 'Eggs or live birth?' },
  { id: 'strategies', label: 'Many young or few?' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'model-limits', label: 'What the model simplifies' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., & Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, Sections 43.1–43.2: Reproduction Methods; Fertilization. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/43-1-reproduction-methods' },
  { text: 'Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., & Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, Section 43.6: Fertilization and Early Embryonic Development. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/43-6-fertilization-and-early-embryonic-development' },
  { text: 'Gilbert, S. F., & Barresi, M. J. F. (2016). Developmental Biology (11th ed.). Sinauer Associates.' },
  { text: 'Gullan, P. J., & Cranston, P. S. (2014). The Insects: An Outline of Entomology (5th ed.). Wiley-Blackwell.' },
  { text: 'Maynard Smith, J. (1978). The Evolution of Sex. Cambridge University Press.' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="zoology"
  topic="life-cycles"
  description="Interactive animal life cycles simulator: step through the life cycles of a butterfly, grasshopper, frog and chicken, watch fertilisation and early development, and compare sexual and asexual reproduction and egg-laying with live birth."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={lifeCycleQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      A caterpillar that dissolves itself and re-emerges with wings. A tadpole that swaps gills for lungs. A chick that grows
      inside a shell from a single cell in just three weeks. Animals have found astonishingly different ways to make the
      next generation. Step through their life cycles, watch an embryo take shape, and test why so many animals bother with
      two parents.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <LifeCycleSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>In <strong>Life cycles</strong>, choose an animal and use <strong>Next stage</strong>, <strong>Back</strong>, the stage buttons or a click on the circle to move through its life cycle. Press <strong>Start</strong> to play it automatically.</li>
      <li>Read what changes at each stage, and compare the butterfly (complete metamorphosis) with the grasshopper (incomplete). The chart shows how many eggs survive to each stage.</li>
      <li>In <strong>Embryo</strong>, step from fertilisation to the first organs, and compare a frog (external fertilisation) with a chicken (internal).</li>
      <li>In <strong>Strategies</strong>, press <strong>Start</strong> to grow an asexual and a sexual population, then press <strong>Disease outbreak</strong>. Compare where embryos develop in egg-laying and live-bearing animals.</li>
      <li>Click any stage, cell or panel for an explanation. <strong>Reset</strong> returns to the butterfly egg.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="why">Why reproduction matters</h2>
  <p>
    Every animal eventually dies, so a species survives only if its members reproduce. Reproduction passes genes to the
    next generation and keeps the population going. A <strong>life cycle</strong> is the series of stages an animal passes
    through from a fertilised egg (or a bud) to an adult that can reproduce itself.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sexual-asexual">Sexual and asexual reproduction</h2>
  <p>
    In <strong>asexual reproduction</strong> one parent produces offspring genetically identical to itself. Hydra grow
    buds that break off; flatworms split in two; some sea stars regrow a whole body from a lost arm; and in
    <strong>parthenogenesis</strong> females produce young from unfertilised eggs, as aphids do all summer and some lizards
    always do. Asexual reproduction is fast and needs no mate. In <strong>sexual reproduction</strong>, two parents each
    contribute a gamete (sperm or egg), and their genes are mixed, so every offspring is different. Half the population
    (the males) does not give birth, which is costly, but the variety helps populations survive diseases and changing
    environments. Many animals, like aphids, switch between the two.
  </p>

  <h2 id="fertilisation">Fertilisation and early development</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Fertilisation</strong> is the fusion of a sperm and an egg. Each gamete carries one set of chromosomes, made
    by meiosis, so the fertilised egg, the <strong>zygote</strong>, has two sets. Many aquatic animals, such as frogs and
    most fish, use <strong>external fertilisation</strong>, releasing eggs and sperm into the water. Land animals mostly use
    <strong>internal fertilisation</strong>, which protects the gametes from drying out. The zygote then divides rapidly
    (<strong>cleavage</strong>) into a ball of cells, which becomes hollow (the <strong>blastula</strong>). During
    <strong>gastrulation</strong> cells move inwards to form three germ layers, ectoderm, mesoderm and endoderm, which give
    rise to all the tissues and organs.
  </p>

  <h2 id="metamorphosis">Metamorphosis in insects</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Stages</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Complete</strong></td><td>Egg → larva → pupa → adult</td><td>Butterflies, beetles, flies, bees</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Incomplete</strong></td><td>Egg → nymph → adult</td><td>Grasshoppers, dragonflies, cockroaches</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Insects have a hard exoskeleton that cannot stretch, so they grow by <strong>moulting</strong>. In complete
    metamorphosis the larva is a feeding stage that looks nothing like the adult; inside the <strong>pupa</strong>, its body
    is largely broken down and rebuilt. Larvae and adults often eat different food, so they do not compete. Most insect
    species develop this way. In incomplete metamorphosis, <strong>nymphs</strong> resemble small adults and change
    gradually.
  </p>

  <h2 id="frog">The frog’s double life</h2>
  <p>
    Frogs lay jelly-coated eggs in water, fertilised externally. The <strong>tadpole</strong> lives like a fish, with gills
    and a tail, and mostly eats algae. During <strong>metamorphosis</strong>, controlled by thyroid hormones, it grows legs,
    develops lungs, absorbs its tail, and its gut changes for a diet of insects. The adult lives on land but must return to
    water to breed. The name amphibian means “double life”.
  </p>

  <h2 id="chicken">Birds and the shelled egg</h2>
  <p>
    Reptiles, birds and mammals have an <strong>amniotic egg</strong>: membranes enclose the embryo in fluid, store waste
    and exchange gases. In birds and most reptiles, a shell protects the egg and a yolk feeds the embryo, so eggs can be
    laid on dry land. A chicken embryo develops in 21 days and hatches as a small version of the adult: this is
    <strong>direct development</strong>, with no larva.
  </p>

  <h2 id="birth">Eggs or live birth?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Oviparous</strong> animals lay eggs that develop outside the mother: most insects, fish, amphibians, reptiles, all birds, and the egg-laying mammals (platypus and echidnas).</li>
    <li><strong>Ovoviviparous</strong> animals keep eggs inside the body until they hatch, so the young are born live, but are fed mainly by yolk: some sharks, many vipers and garter snakes.</li>
    <li><strong>Viviparous</strong> animals nourish the embryo inside the mother, in most mammals through a <strong>placenta</strong>: most mammals, and some sharks, lizards and fish.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Biologists now often describe these as points on a range, because many species fall between the categories. In
    seahorses it is even the male that carries the developing young.
  </p>

  <h2 id="strategies">Many young or few?</h2>
  <p>
    A frog may lay 2,000 eggs and give no care; a wild hen lays a handful and guards her chicks; an elephant has one calf
    every few years and raises it for years. Producing many cheap offspring and producing a few well-protected ones are both
    successful strategies. Because most young die, a population stays stable when, on average, each pair leaves about two
    offspring that survive to breed.
  </p>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“A pupa is resting.”</strong> It is the stage of greatest change.</li>
    <li><strong>“All insects have a caterpillar stage.”</strong> Only insects with complete metamorphosis have larvae; grasshoppers have nymphs.</li>
    <li><strong>“Every egg a hen lays could become a chick.”</strong> Only fertilised eggs that are incubated develop.</li>
    <li><strong>“Asexual reproduction only happens in plants and bacteria.”</strong> Many animals reproduce asexually.</li>
    <li><strong>“All mammals give birth to live young.”</strong> The platypus and echidnas lay eggs.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="model-limits">What the model simplifies</h2>
  <p>
    The drawings are simplified and not to scale, and each animal represents a large group. Durations are typical ranges for
    one example species and depend on temperature and food. The survival numbers are illustrative orders of magnitude, not
    measurements. The development steps are generalised: frogs and chickens differ in how their eggs divide, because of
    their very different amounts of yolk. The population model uses two genes with three variants each, equal numbers of
    young per female, a fixed space limit, and a disease that targets the most common type; real populations, mutation
    rates and diseases are far more complex.
  </p>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Reproduction keeps species going; life cycles describe the stages from egg to breeding adult.</li>
    <li>Asexual reproduction is fast but makes identical offspring; sexual reproduction makes varied offspring.</li>
    <li>Fertilisation joins two haploid gametes into a diploid zygote, which divides and forms germ layers and organs.</li>
    <li>Complete metamorphosis has a pupa; incomplete metamorphosis has nymphs; frogs change from aquatic tadpoles to land-living adults.</li>
    <li>Animals may lay eggs (oviparous), keep eggs inside until they hatch (ovoviviparous) or nourish embryos inside the mother (viviparous).</li>
  </ul>
</SimulatorLayout>
